<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paris Lodron</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1586">1586</a>–<a href="/years/1653">1653</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Lodron was prince-archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> from 1619 to 1653. Through careful neutrality and new fortifications he kept the archbishopric out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. He consecrated the rebuilt Salzburg Cathedral in 1628 and founded the University of Salzburg in 1622.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paris-lodron.jpg" alt="Paris Lodron" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joannes Jenet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paris_Lodron_G_1062_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Paris Lodron was born on 13 February 1586 at Castelnuovo di Noarna in Nogaredo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a>, the son of Count Nicolò Lodron, an imperial colonel and governor of the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, and Dorothea von Welsperg. At eleven he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trento</a> to study theology, then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, and he completed his studies with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingolstadt">Ingolstadt</a> in 1604. Ordained a priest in March 1614, he became provost of the cathedral chapter and president of the archbishop's exchequer at the request of Prince-Archbishop Markus Sittich von Hohenems.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Sittich's death on 9 October 1619, Lodron was elected his successor on 13 November and received his episcopal ordination on 23 May of the next year. Sittich had refused to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a>, which allowed Lodron to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> and turn to his own lands. The master builder Santino Solari gave the town and country modern bastion fortifications: a belt of five large bastions round the Neustadt, the rocks of the Mönchsberg cut back to serve as natural walls, and an enlarged Hohensalzburg Fortress.</p><p>On 23 July 1622 he appointed the scholar Albert Keuslin first rector of the Benedictine University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, which Emperor Ferdinand II raised from Keuslin's Akademisches Gymnasium by a resolution of 8 October. A federation of Benedictine abbeys from Salzburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> maintained it, teaching theology, philosophy, law and medicine. Lodron also completed and decorated the cathedral begun under Sittich and consecrated it on 25 September 1628 with an eight-day baroque festival, and he founded collegiate houses at Laufen and Tittmoning.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lodron died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> on 15 December 1653 and was succeeded by Guidobald von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thun">Thun</a> und Hohenstein. Unlike some of his predecessors he kept no mistress, but he provided generously for the first and second sons of his brother Christoph, building two palaces for them in the centre of Salzburg, the Primogeniturpalast and the Sekundogeniturpalast, which still stood in the 21st century. The university is sometimes called the Paris-Lodron-Universität after him, and the Paris-Lodron-Straße near Mirabell Palace marks the border between the Altstadt and the Neustadt.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paris Lodron” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wolf Dietrich von Raitenau and Markus Sittikus rebuilt Salzburg in Italian baroque style, and <b>Paris Lodron</b> kept it out of the Thirty Years War.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-archbishopric-of-salzburg">Prince-Archbishopric of Salzburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Castelnuovo di Noarna, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Mirabell Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.91,11.02&amp;year=1629" aria-label="Castelnuovo di Noarna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4811 546 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Paris Lodron"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5261 816h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5261 816h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.91,11.02&amp;year=1629">What was here in 1629? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
